post_install() {
  # Fixed loopback ports cannot be shared by every user manager (including
  # display-manager greeter accounts). Hearthdeck launchers start this target
  # explicitly for the active user.
  systemctl --global disable hearthdeck.target 2>/dev/null || true
  udevadm control --reload-rules 2>/dev/null || true
  modprobe uinput 2>/dev/null || true
  udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=misc --sysname-match=uinput 2>/dev/null || true
  # The optional romm.service runs `podman-compose up`, and podman's default
  # (pasta) networking opens /dev/net/tun. Same shape of need as uinput above:
  # best-effort load so a host that ships tun as a module gets it, and never a
  # hard failure on hosts where tun is built in or absent.
  modprobe tun 2>/dev/null || true
  if ! grep -qxF 'Include = /etc/pacman.d/hearthdeck.conf' /etc/pacman.conf; then
    cat >>/etc/pacman.conf <<'EOF'

# BEGIN HEARTHDECK PACMAN REPOSITORY
Include = /etc/pacman.d/hearthdeck.conf
# END HEARTHDECK PACMAN REPOSITORY
EOF
  fi

  cat <<'EOF'
Hearthdeck installs systemd user units for its API daemon and host bridge, and
a minimal "Hearthdeck Kiosk" Wayland session with no desktop shell.

The Hearthdeck launchers and sessions start the local-service target for the
active user. Select Hearthdeck Kiosk in the display manager to run Gamescope
with Hearthdeck as its only child. To start the services immediately without
launching Hearthdeck, run as that user:

  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user start hearthdeck.target

To skip the login screen entirely - the usual setup for a box under a TV with
no keyboard - on a greetd-based display manager:

  sudo /usr/lib/hearthdeck/hearthdeck-autologin enable

It adds greetd's [initial_session] to the config the running display manager
uses; run it with --help for other users, other session commands, or to get the
login screen back with `disable`. See KIOSK-SESSION.md for what it changes.

The launcher pairs with the loopback daemon automatically. Optional LAN/TLS
configuration belongs in ~/.config/hearthdeck/daemon.env; see
/usr/share/doc/hearthdeck/daemon.env.example.

The Hearthdeck session starts the Podman Compose RomM deployment at
/mnt/external/romM/podman-compose.yaml when that file exists. To override the
path, copy /usr/share/doc/hearthdeck/romm.env.example to
~/.config/hearthdeck/romm.env and edit ROMM_COMPOSE_FILE.

Combined session logs are written to ~/hearthdeck.log.
Run /usr/lib/hearthdeck/linux-acceptance --require-romm after upgrading to
verify the installed services and RomM stack.

Future Hearthdeck package updates arrive through `pacman -Syu`. The GitHub
Pages repository is unsigned and should not be enabled on systems that require
signed packages.

If Hearthdeck was previously installed from a source checkout, move its copied
unit files out of ~/.config/systemd/user/ first. Those files override the
package units in /usr/lib/systemd/user. Preserve customizations as drop-ins.
EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}

pre_remove() {
  sed -i '/^# BEGIN HEARTHDECK PACMAN REPOSITORY$/,/^# END HEARTHDECK PACMAN REPOSITORY$/d' /etc/pacman.conf
}

post_remove() {
  systemctl --global disable hearthdeck.target

  cat <<'EOF'
If this was the final Hearthdeck package removal, disable its user services:
  systemctl --user disable --now hearthdeck.target hearthdeck-bridge.socket
EOF
}
